#: source/development/data-modeling.md:1
msgid "Data Modeling Guidelines"
msgstr ""

#: source/development/data-modeling.md:3
msgid ""
"Nix consumes and produces JSON and attribute sets in a variety of contexts. "
"These guidelines ensure consistent practices for our interfaces, for ease of "
"use, and so that experience in one part carries over to another."
msgstr ""

#: source/development/data-modeling.md:6
msgid ""
"For these guidelines, we will use JSON terminology, but they apply equally "
"well to new attribute set interfaces (primops, etc.). Note that these are "
"guidelines first and foremost. Exceptions include:"
msgstr ""

#: source/development/data-modeling.md:8
msgid "Feature testing: e.g., it is OK to do `builtins?frobnicate`."
msgstr ""

#: source/development/data-modeling.md:9
msgid ""
"Compatibility: we generally do not change stable interfaces just to make "
"them comply. New replacements can be added with care."
msgstr ""

#: source/development/data-modeling.md:11
msgid "Extensibility"
msgstr ""

#: source/development/data-modeling.md:13
msgid ""
"The schema of JSON input and output should allow for backwards compatible "
"extension. This section explains how to achieve this."
msgstr ""

#: source/development/data-modeling.md:16
msgid ""
"Two definitions are helpful here, because while JSON only defines one \"key-"
"value\" object type, we use it to cover two use cases:"
msgstr ""

#: source/development/data-modeling.md:18
msgid ""
"**dictionary**: a map from names to value that all have the same type. In C+"
"+ this would be a `std::map` with string keys."
msgstr ""

#: source/development/data-modeling.md:21
msgid ""
"**record**: a fixed set of attributes each with their own type. In C++, this "
"would be represented by a `struct`."
msgstr ""

#: source/development/data-modeling.md:24
msgid ""
"It is best not to mix these use cases, as that may lead to incompatibilities "
"when the schema changes. For example, adding a record field to a dictionary "
"breaks consumers that assume all JSON object fields to have the same meaning "
"and type, and dictionary items with a colliding name can not be represented "
"anymore."
msgstr ""

#: source/development/data-modeling.md:27
msgid "This leads to the following guidelines:"
msgstr ""

#: source/development/data-modeling.md:29
msgid "The top-level (root) value must be a record."
msgstr ""

#: source/development/data-modeling.md:31
msgid "Otherwise, one can not change the structure of a command's output."
msgstr ""

#: source/development/data-modeling.md:33
msgid "The value of a dictionary item must be a record."
msgstr ""

#: source/development/data-modeling.md:35
msgid "Otherwise, the item type can not be extended."
msgstr ""

#: source/development/data-modeling.md:37
msgid "List items should be records."
msgstr ""

#: source/development/data-modeling.md:39
msgid "Otherwise, one can not change the structure of the list items."
msgstr ""

#: source/development/data-modeling.md:41
msgid ""
"If the order of the items does not matter, and each item has a unique key "
"that is a string, consider representing the list as a dictionary instead. If "
"the order of the items needs to be preserved, return a list of records."
msgstr ""

#: source/development/data-modeling.md:44
msgid "Streaming JSON should return records."
msgstr ""

#: source/development/data-modeling.md:46
msgid ""
"An example of a streaming JSON format is [JSON lines](https://jsonlines."
"org/), where each line represents a JSON value. These JSON values can be "
"considered top-level values or list items, and they must be records."
msgstr ""

#: source/development/data-modeling.md:49
#: source/development/data-modeling.md:102
msgid "Examples"
msgstr ""

#: source/development/data-modeling.md:51
msgid "This is bad, because all keys must be assumed to be store types:"
msgstr ""

#: source/development/data-modeling.md:55
#: source/development/data-modeling.md:65
msgid "\"local\""
msgstr ""

#: source/development/data-modeling.md:56
msgid "\"remote\""
msgstr ""

#: source/development/data-modeling.md:57
msgid "\"http\""
msgstr ""

#: source/development/data-modeling.md:61
msgid ""
"This is good, because the it is extensible at the root, and is somewhat self-"
"documenting:"
msgstr ""

#: source/development/data-modeling.md:65
msgid "\"storeTypes\""
msgstr ""

#: source/development/data-modeling.md:66
msgid "\"pluginSupport\""
msgstr ""

#: source/development/data-modeling.md:70
msgid ""
"While the dictionary of store types seems like a very complete response at "
"first, a use case may arise that warrants returning additional information. "
"For example, the presence of plugin support may be crucial information for a "
"client to proceed when their desired store type is missing."
msgstr ""

#: source/development/data-modeling.md:75
msgid "The following representation is bad because it is not extensible:"
msgstr ""

#: source/development/data-modeling.md:78
#: source/development/data-modeling.md:84
#: source/development/data-modeling.md:93
msgid "\"outputs\""
msgstr ""

#: source/development/data-modeling.md:78
msgid "\"out\" \"bin\""
msgstr ""

#: source/development/data-modeling.md:81
msgid ""
"However, simply converting everything to records is not enough, because the "
"order of outputs must be preserved:"
msgstr ""

#: source/development/data-modeling.md:84
#: source/development/data-modeling.md:93
msgid "\"bin\""
msgstr ""

#: source/development/data-modeling.md:84
#: source/development/data-modeling.md:93
msgid "\"out\""
msgstr ""

#: source/development/data-modeling.md:87
msgid ""
"The first item is the default output. Deriving this information from the "
"outputs ordering is not great, but this is how Nix currently happens to "
"work. While it is possible for a JSON parser to preserve the order of "
"fields, we can not rely on this capability to be present in all JSON "
"libraries."
msgstr ""

#: source/development/data-modeling.md:90
msgid "This representation is extensible and preserves the ordering:"
msgstr ""

#: source/development/data-modeling.md:93
msgid "\"outputName\""
msgstr ""

#: source/development/data-modeling.md:96
msgid "Self-describing values"
msgstr ""

#: source/development/data-modeling.md:98
msgid ""
"As described in the previous section, it's crucial that schemas can be "
"extended with new fields without breaking compatibility. However, that "
"should _not_ mean we use the presence/absence of fields to indicate optional "
"information _within_ a version of the schema. Instead, always include the "
"field, and use `null` to indicate the \"nothing\" case."
msgstr ""

#: source/development/data-modeling.md:104
msgid "Here are two JSON objects:"
msgstr ""

#: source/development/data-modeling.md:108
#: source/development/data-modeling.md:113
#: source/development/data-modeling.md:126
#: source/development/data-modeling.md:129
msgid "\"foo\""
msgstr ""

#: source/development/data-modeling.md:114
#: source/development/data-modeling.md:129
msgid "\"bar\""
msgstr ""

#: source/development/data-modeling.md:118
msgid ""
"Since they differ in which fields they contain, they should _not_ both be "
"valid values of the same schema. At most, they can match two different "
"schemas where the second (with `foo` and `bar`) is considered a newer "
"version of the first (with just `foo`). Within each version, all fields are "
"mandatory (always `foo`, and always `foo` and `bar`). Only _between_ each "
"version, `bar` gets added as a new mandatory field."
msgstr ""

#: source/development/data-modeling.md:123
msgid "Here are another two JSON objects:"
msgstr ""

#: source/development/data-modeling.md:132
msgid ""
"Since they both contain a `foo` field, they could be valid values of the "
"same schema. The schema would have `foo` has an optional field, which is "
"either `null` or an object where `bar` is an integer."
msgstr ""
